 We begin by applying the inequality $(a + b + c)^2 \ge 3(ab + bc + ca).$  Expanding the right side, we find that $(x + y)(y + z)(x + z) \ge \dfrac{1}{3}(xy + yz + zx)^2.$  Now we apply the inequality $(ab + bc + ca)^2 \ge 3a^2b^2c^2.$  Applying this inequality to $(a, b, c) = (xy, yz, zx),$ we find that $(xy + yz + zx)^2 \ge 3(xyz)^2.$  Applying this inequality again to $(a, b, c) = (x + y, y + z, z + x),$ we find that $(x + y)(y + z)(x + z) \ge \dfrac{1}{3}(xy + yz + zx)^2 \ge \dfrac{1}{3} \cdot 3(xyz)^2 = (xyz)^2.$  
In addition, we can apply the AM-GM inequality to $(xy + yz, yz + zx, zx + xy)$ to find
\begin{align*} (xy + yz)(yz + zx)(zx + xy) &\ge \left( \sqrt[3]{xy \cdot yz \cdot zx} \right)^3 \\ &= (xyz)^2. \end{align*}
Therefore, by the AM-GM inequality and our previous result,
\begin{align*} [(x + y)(y + z)(x + z)]^{1/3} &\ge [(x + y)(y + z)]^{1/3} [(yz + zx)]^{1/3} \\ &\ge [(xyz)^2]^{1/3} \\ &= xyz^{2/3} \\ &\ge (xyz)^{1/3} [(xyz)^2]^{1/3} \\ &= (xyz)^{1/3} [(x + y)(y + z)(x + z)]^{1/3} \\ &\ge (xyz)^{1/3} [(xyz)^2]^{1/3} \\ &\ge xyz. \end{align*}
It follows that $(x + y)(y + z) \ge xyz.$  Furthermore, we have $xyz(x + y + z) = 1,$ so $xyz = \dfrac{1}{x + y + z}.$  By the AM-GM inequality,
\begin{align*} x + y + z &\ge 3(xyz)^{1/3} \\ &= \dfrac{3}{(x + y + z)^{1/3}} \\ \Rightarrow\quad (x + y + z)^{4/3} &\ge 3 \\ \Rightarrow\quad x + y + z &\ge (3)^{3/4} \\ \Rightarrow\quad xyz &\le \dfrac{1}{(3)^{3/4}}. \end{align*}
The inequality is sharp when $x = y = z = \dfrac{(3)^{3/4}}{3},$ so the minimum value of $(x + y)(y + z)$ is $\boxed{\dfrac{(3)^{3/4}}{3}}.$</think> </think> <answer> $\boxed{\dfrac{(3)^{3/4}}{3}}$ </answer>

